<main id="main"><section class="viewer-top shell" aria-labelledby="chapter-title"><a class="back-link" href="exhibition.php#chapters">← Back to chapters</a><div class="viewer-progress-meta"><span>THE BIRTH OF THE INTERNET</span><span>CHAPTER 04 OF 05</span></div><div class="viewer-progress" role="progressbar" aria-label="Exhibition chapter progress" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="5" aria-valuenow="4"><span style="width:80%"></span></div><div class="viewer-heading-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-line"></span> 1991–1999 / THE WEB ARRIVES</p><h1 id="chapter-title">The Web<br><em>Goes Public.</em></h1></div><p class="viewer-intro">The internet carried data. The World Wide Web made it possible to explore linked pages — and browsers opened that world to millions.</p></div></section>
<section class="chapter-feature-stage"><div class="shell"><div class="feature-graphic browser-graphic" role="img" aria-label="Stylized early web browser, illustrating pages linked by hyperlinks"><div class="retro-browser"><div class="retro-toolbar"><span>● ○ ○</span><span>World Wide Web / 1993</span></div><div class="retro-address">http://info.example/</div><div class="retro-content"><small>WELCOME TO THE WEB</small><strong>A world of<br>linked ideas.</strong><span>Explore → <u>Another page</u> &nbsp; / &nbsp; <u>More knowledge</u></span></div></div></div><div class="stage-caption"><span>FIG. 04 — THE BROWSER AS A WINDOW ON THE WEB</span><span>ORIGINAL INTERPRETIVE ILLUSTRATION</span></div></div></section>
<section class="viewer-story shell"><div class="viewer-side-note"><span class="eyebrow">01 / A WEB OF DOCUMENTS</span><span>An idea for sharing knowledge took shape.</span></div><div class="viewer-prose"><h2>Follow the<br><em>link.</em></h2><p class='dropcap'>In 1989, Tim Berners-Lee proposed an information-management system at CERN. With collaborators, he developed core web technologies including HTML, HTTP, and URLs, along with an early browser and server.</p><p>The World Wide Web is not the same thing as the internet: it is a service built on top of internet infrastructure. Its central idea is simple and powerful — documents connected by hyperlinks.</p><p>In 1993, CERN made the web software available on a royalty-free basis, helping accelerate its spread beyond research institutions.</p></div></section>
<section class="artifact-band"><div class="shell artifact-grid"><div class="artifact-visual later-artifact"><div class="artifact-label">ARTIFACT / A HYPERLINK</div><div class="exhibit-mini-visual link-visual"><span>DOCUMENT 01</span><b>↗ LINK ↗</b><span>DOCUMENT 02</span></div><div class="artifact-bottom">EXHIBIT / ORIGINAL INTERPRETIVE GRAPHIC</div></div><div class="artifact-copy"><p class="eyebrow">ARTIFACT / A HYPERLINK</p><h2>A page becomes<br><em>a doorway.</em></h2><p>Text could point to another document anywhere on the web. Each click could take a visitor into a new subject, a new institution, or an entirely new corner of the network.</p><p class="artifact-caption">Conceptual diagram created for this prototype; not a historical reproduction.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="viewer-story shell story-second"><div class="viewer-side-note"><span class="eyebrow">02 / THE BROWSER ERA</span><span>The web becomes a public experience.</span></div><div class="viewer-prose"><h2>A window into<br><em>everything.</em></h2><p>Graphical browsers helped make the web more approachable. Mosaic appeared in 1993, and Netscape Navigator followed in 1994. Browsing moved from specialized environments toward everyday computers.</p><p>The late 1990s brought web directories, search engines, personal homepages, online stores, and the first wave of web businesses. The web was exciting, uneven, experimental — and unmistakably growing.</p></div></section>
<section class="viewer-ending shell" aria-label="Chapter navigation"><div><p class="eyebrow">CHAPTER 04 COMPLETE</p><h2>The world starts<br><em>clicking.</em></h2><p>Next: the network moves from desktop computers into the rhythms of daily life.</p></div><div class="viewer-ending-actions"><span class="viewer-next-muted">UP NEXT · CHAPTER 05 / ALWAYS CONNECTED</span><a class="primary-link" href="chapter-5.php">Continue to Chapter 05 →</a><a class="viewer-secondary-link" href="chapter-3.php">← Previous chapter</a><a class="viewer-secondary-link" href="exhibition.php#chapters">Browse all chapters ↗</a><a class="viewer-secondary-link" href="#main">Back to chapter start ↑</a></div></section></main><footer class="footer"><div class="shell footer-inner"><span class="footer-brand">exhibit<span>.</span></span><span>Made for the endlessly curious.</span><span>© <?=date('Y')?> Exhibit</span></div></footer></body></html>